From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:48:08 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: tas2764: Keep SDZ high across sleep only within an SDZ
 group

"ASoC: tas2764: keep SDZ high across system sleep" changed suspend and
resume for every TAS2764 and SN012776 amp, but it has only run on the
J616s, whose six amps share one SDZ supply as an SDZ group. The M1 and
M2 machines also use this driver and have not been tested with the
amps left in software shutdown across sleep.

Leave SDZ high only for amps in an SDZ group. Amps outside a group
drop SDZ on suspend and raise it on resume, as before. Losing the
page-0xfd quirk writes over sleep on those machines stays a separate
fix.

When a group has failed, its supply was forced off and no enable is
left for codec_remove() to balance, so skip the disable there.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
(cherry picked from commit 946d7aa83fbb3fdcf07986238a8d19f39b69577c)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c | 38 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
index 47784cc1d1..91c5ebbd52 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/tas2764.c
@@ -269,11 +269,15 @@ static int tas2764_update_pwr_ctrl(struct tas2764_priv *tas2764)
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_PM
 /*
- * System sleep leaves the SDZ line high: the amp waits in software shutdown, as it does between
- * streams, and keeps its registers. Pulling SDZ low here meant a power-on reset at resume, and
- * soc_resume_deferred() unmutes the amps of a running stream a few milliseconds later: their I/V
- * sense then read a negative average power for several seconds, and the uncached page-0xfd quirk
- * writes were lost. After cold boot no amp is unmuted that fresh out of reset.
+ * Members of an SDZ group keep the shared SDZ line high across system sleep: the amp waits in
+ * software shutdown, as it does between streams, and keeps its registers. Pulling SDZ low here
+ * meant a power-on reset at resume, and soc_resume_deferred() unmutes the amps of a running stream
+ * a few milliseconds later: their I/V sense then read a negative average power for several
+ * seconds, and the uncached page-0xfd quirk writes were lost. After cold boot no amp is unmuted
+ * that fresh out of reset.
+ *
+ * Amps outside a group keep the earlier behaviour (SDZ low across sleep) until the change has run
+ * on the machines that use them.
  */
 static int tas2764_codec_suspend(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 {
@@ -287,6 +291,13 @@ static int tas2764_codec_suspend(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return ret;
 
+	if (!tas2764->sdz_group) {
+		if (tas2764->sdz_gpio)
+			gpiod_set_value_cansleep(tas2764->sdz_gpio, 0);
+
+		regulator_disable(tas2764->sdz_reg);
+	}
+
 	regcache_cache_only(tas2764->regmap, true);
 	regcache_mark_dirty(tas2764->regmap);
 
@@ -300,6 +311,19 @@ static int tas2764_codec_resume(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 	struct tas2764_priv *tas2764 = snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(component);
 	int ret;
 
+	if (!tas2764->sdz_group) {
+		ret = regulator_enable(tas2764->sdz_reg);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(tas2764->dev, "Failed to enable regulator\n");
+			return ret;
+		}
+
+		if (tas2764->sdz_gpio)
+			gpiod_set_value_cansleep(tas2764->sdz_gpio, 1);
+
+		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+	}
+
 	regcache_cache_only(tas2764->regmap, false);
 
 	ret = regcache_sync(tas2764->regmap);
@@ -983,6 +1007,10 @@ static void tas2764_codec_remove(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 {
 	struct tas2764_priv *tas2764 = snd_soc_component_get_drvdata(component);
 
+	/* A failed group's supply was forced off: there is no enable left to balance. */
+	if (tas2764_group_failed(tas2764))
+		return;
+
 	regulator_disable(tas2764->sdz_reg);
 }
 
